### What is columnar layout?

The fundamental idea is storing data by column instead of by row. A row-oriented format like CSV stores all fields of row 1, then all fields of row 2. Parquet flips this: it stores all values of column A together, then all of column B. The immediate payoff for analytics is column pruning.

```java
package com.bank.formats;

public class ColumnarVsRow {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Row-oriented (CSV):");
        System.out.println("  [T1|SAV2001|15000|2024-10-29|Mumbai]");
        System.out.println("  [T2|CUR3002|25000|2024-10-29|Delhi]");
        System.out.println("  -> SUM(amount): must read ALL columns");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Column-oriented (Parquet):");
        System.out.println("  txn_id: [T1, T2, T3, ...]");
        System.out.println("  amount: [15000, 25000, 30000, ...]");
        System.out.println("  date:   [Oct-29, Oct-29, ...]");
        System.out.println("  -> SUM(amount): reads ONLY amount column");
    }
}
```

```text
Row-oriented (CSV):
  [T1|SAV2001|15000|2024-10-29|Mumbai]
  [T2|CUR3002|25000|2024-10-29|Delhi]
  -> SUM(amount): must read ALL columns

Column-oriented (Parquet):
  txn_id: [T1, T2, T3, ...]
  amount: [15000, 25000, 30000, ...]
  date:   [Oct-29, Oct-29, ...]
  -> SUM(amount): reads ONLY amount column
```

### How does column pruning work?

Column pruning means reading only the columns a query needs. On a 100-column transaction table, if the query only selects amount and date, Parquet reads just those two columns' bytes and never touches the other 98, cutting I/O by orders of magnitude.

```python
# Column pruning: Spark reads only 2 of 100 columns
df = spark.read.parquet(
    "s3://national-bank-lake/curated/transactions/")
result = df.select("amount", "txn_date") \
    .groupBy("txn_date").sum("amount")

result.explain(True)
# FileScan parquet [amount, txn_date]
# -> only 2 columns read, 98 skipped
```

```text
== Physical Plan ==
HashAggregate(keys=[txn_date], functions=[sum(amount)])
+- FileScan parquet [amount, txn_date]  <-- only 2 of 100 columns
```

### How does predicate pushdown work?

Parquet organizes data into row groups, and within each row group each column chunk stores min and max values. If a query filters `amount > 100000` and a row group's max amount is 50000, the engine skips that entire row group without decoding it.

```java
package com.bank.formats;

public class PredicatePushdown {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Filter: amount > 100000");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Row group 0: min=500, max=50000");
        System.out.println("  -> max < 100000, SKIP (no decompress)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Row group 1: min=45000, max=200000");
        System.out.println("  -> max >= 100000, READ");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Row group 2: min=100, max=30000");
        System.out.println("  -> max < 100000, SKIP");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Result: read 1 of 3 row groups.");
    }
}
```

```text
Filter: amount > 100000

Row group 0: min=500, max=50000
  -> max < 100000, SKIP (no decompress)

Row group 1: min=45000, max=200000
  -> max >= 100000, READ

Row group 2: min=100, max=30000
  -> max < 100000, SKIP

Result: read 1 of 3 row groups.
```

### How does compression work in Parquet?

Because a column holds values of the same type and often with repetition, Parquet applies encodings like dictionary encoding and run-length encoding, then a codec like Snappy or Zstandard. Similar values compress dramatically better than mixed-type rows.

```java
package com.bank.formats;

public class ParquetCompression {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Encoding examples:");
        System.out.println("  branch column: [Mumbai, Mumbai, Delhi,"
            + " Delhi, Mumbai]");
        System.out.println("  Dictionary: {0=Mumbai, 1=Delhi}");
        System.out.println("  Encoded: [0, 0, 1, 1, 0]");
        System.out.println("  -> 5 ints vs 5 strings");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  date column: [2024-10-29 x 1000]");
        System.out.println("  Run-length: (2024-10-29, 1000)");
        System.out.println("  -> 1 value + count vs 1000 values");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Typical compression: 5-10x smaller.");
    }
}
```

```text
Encoding examples:
  branch column: [Mumbai, Mumbai, Delhi, Delhi, Mumbai]
  Dictionary: {0=Mumbai, 1=Delhi}
  Encoded: [0, 0, 1, 1, 0]
  -> 5 ints vs 5 strings

  date column: [2024-10-29 x 1000]
  Run-length: (2024-10-29, 1000)
  -> 1 value + count vs 1000 values

Typical compression: 5-10x smaller.
```

### What is the Parquet file structure?

A Parquet file has a header (magic bytes), one or more row groups, and a footer. The footer holds the full schema and per-column statistics. Engines read the footer first to plan the scan.

```text
Parquet File Structure
┌────────────────────────────────┐
│ Header: PAR1 (magic bytes)     │
├────────────────────────────────┤
│ Row Group 0                    │
│   Column Chunk: txn_id         │
│   Column Chunk: amount         │
│   Column Chunk: branch         │
│   (each chunk has pages +      │
│    min/max statistics)         │
├────────────────────────────────┤
│ Row Group 1                    │
│   Column Chunk: txn_id         │
│   Column Chunk: amount         │
│   Column Chunk: branch         │
├────────────────────────────────┤
│ Footer                         │
│   Schema (column names, types) │
│   Row group locations          │
│   Per-column min/max stats     │
├────────────────────────────────┤
│ Footer Length (4 bytes)        │
│ Footer: PAR1 (magic bytes)     │
└────────────────────────────────┘
```

```java
package com.bank.formats;

public class ParquetStructure {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("1. Engine reads footer first.");
        System.out.println("2. Footer has schema + statistics.");
        System.out.println("3. Engine plans: which columns,"
            + " which row groups.");
        System.out.println("4. Reads only needed data.");
    }
}
```

```text
1. Engine reads footer first.
2. Footer has schema + statistics.
3. Engine plans: which columns, which row groups.
4. Reads only needed data.
```

### How is Parquet different from CSV?

| Aspect | CSV | Parquet |
|---|---|---|
| Layout | Row-oriented | Column-oriented |
| Schema | No (or header row) | Embedded in footer |
| Types | All strings | Typed (int, decimal, etc.) |
| Compression | Weak | Strong (dict, RLE + codec) |
| Column pruning | No (read all) | Yes (read only needed) |
| Predicate pushdown | No | Yes (min/max stats) |
| Human-readable | Yes | No (binary) |
| Best for | Small data, debugging | Analytics at scale |

```java
package com.bank.formats;

public class CsvVsParquet {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("CSV: 100-column table, SUM(amount)");
        System.out.println("  Reads ALL 100 columns. No skip.");
        System.out.println("  Size: 10 GB uncompressed.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Parquet: same table, SUM(amount)");
        System.out.println("  Reads ONLY amount column. 99 skipped.");
        System.out.println("  Size: 1.5 GB compressed.");
    }
}
```

```text
CSV: 100-column table, SUM(amount)
  Reads ALL 100 columns. No skip.
  Size: 10 GB uncompressed.

Parquet: same table, SUM(amount)
  Reads ONLY amount column. 99 skipped.
  Size: 1.5 GB compressed.
```

### What are the limitations of Parquet?

Parquet is designed for write-once, read-many analytical use, not for frequent single-row updates. It pairs with lake table formats like Delta Lake and Iceberg that add ACID transactions and updates on top of Parquet files. It is also poor for retrieving one whole row with all columns, where a row store still wins.

```java
package com.bank.formats;

public class ParquetLimitations {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Parquet strengths:");
        System.out.println("  Wide scans, aggregations, analytics");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Parquet weaknesses:");
        System.out.println("  No in-place UPDATE/DELETE");
        System.out.println("  Poor for single-row full-column fetch");
        System.out.println("  Not human-readable (binary)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Solution: Delta Lake / Iceberg on top");
        System.out.println("  Adds ACID, MERGE, time travel.");
    }
}
```

```text
Parquet strengths:
  Wide scans, aggregations, analytics

Parquet weaknesses:
  No in-place UPDATE/DELETE
  Poor for single-row full-column fetch
  Not human-readable (binary)

Solution: Delta Lake / Iceberg on top
  Adds ACID, MERGE, time travel.
```

